.TH CHG 1 "October 2026"
.SH NAME
chg \- drive the media changer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chg
.BI drive " [" source ]
.br
.B chg
.BI next " drive"
.br
.B chg
.B sets
.br
.B chg
.B drives
.br
.B chg
.B dir
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B chg
.B ls
.RI [ path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I chg
writes its arguments as one command line to
.IR /dev/mediachg ,
the out-of-band media changer, and prints the one-line response.  It
exits 0 on
.B ok
and 1 on
.BR error .
.PP
The grammar is sys/MEDIACHG.md.  A
.I drive
is a controller and a logical unit,
.IB djdma : 0
or
.IB disk1 : 2,
and a
.I source
is an image path, a
.BI blank: name
to cut a fresh one, a
.BI set : vol
from a media set, or
.B empty
to eject.  Naming a drive alone asks what is in it.
.PP
A relative path, and each volume of a set, is resolved against the media
directory, which
.B chg dir
reports and sets.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chg drives                 # ok djdma:0=d8ss.img djdma:1=empty ...
chg djdma:0 dist8:0        # load volume 0 of set dist8
chg djdma:0                # ok dist8:0
chg next djdma:0
chg djdma:1 blank:scratch.img
chg dir /usr/dist
chg ls
chg djdma:0 empty
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
sys/MEDIACHG.md
